  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Generating Renewable and Innovative Designs for Displacement (GRID)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Philippines has been experiencing increased frequency and intensity of extreme weather events due to climate change, with an average 20 typhoons each year. This occurs alongside heavy rainfall, floods, landslides, earthquakes, and volcanic eruptions. The recurrent disasters and displacements due to various hazards, combined with pre-existing vulnerabilities of chronic poverty and underdevelopment, have already depleted their capacities for disaster preparedness and response. With soaring global energy and fuel prices, the current capacity of evacuation centres (ECs) only adds more strain on these seasonally displaced communities, which are among the most impacted by climate change in the world. Building on the expertise gained through years of leading the emergency response and preparedness initiatives, IOM has identified an innovative and potentially viable model for ECs, given the global and local energy access constraints, that will protect and support internally displaced persons as well as highlight a scalable solution. IOM aims to contribute to efficient and reliable access to clean energy by displaced populations in evacuation centres during emergencies. This project seeks to establish a reliable and affordable model that can guide humanitarian actors for wider replication both within and outside the country.</narrative>
  </description>
